  "summary": "Round 3's subprocess.Popen proposal fundamentally misunderstands the execution model it correctly identified: run_script() calls runner.headless(), which is a Python-level AI agent session — not a shell command. You cannot subprocess.Popen a Python method call with start_new_session=True; you'd need to wrap it in a separate CLI entry point or use multiprocessing, adding significant complexity. The sidecar JSON status file approach also introduces its own problems: it creates an ever-growing set of status files that need cleanup, the TUI/WebUI must discover and poll these files (adding UI complexity for an uncommon event), and there's no mechanism to associate a sidecar file back to the feature's history unless something reads it and merges the result — which is the same write-back problem it claims to avoid, just deferred. The simpler, more robust approach is to write the 'script triggered' history entry synchronously before spawning the thread (as rounds 1 and 3 agree), keep the daemon thread but register it in a module-level list, and add an atexit handler that joins all pending script threads with a generous timeout. This avoids the subprocess indirection problem entirely, keeps the audit trail in the feature's own history (where users already look), and only blocks process exit — not normal operation — giving scripts time to complete. For the completion/failure entry, the thread can safely write back because by the time a done_script runs the feature is already in its final 'done' directory and won't be moved again, neutralizing round 2's path-change concern."
